    ('What I found in the code-commandments journal, without changing anything:\n\n1. The journal never asked: its reader_requests table is empty, no request was ever made there.\n\n2. Why: the Go test run was categorised as "unknown", not as a test run. The line was:\nscripts/dev go test -p 2 ./cli/judge/ ... 2>&1 | grep -E "^ok |FAIL|--- FAIL" | head -1\nThe journal recognises go test, but here it is wrapped in the project\'s own script (scripts/dev), so the classifier saw an unknown program. Reader requests, the queue and the rule that a test runs on a line of its own, with no pipe, all fire only for test runs and builds, so none of them came into play.\n\n3. The pipe made it worse: grep and head cut the output to one line, so even with a reader there was nothing to read and no progress to show. In this project such a piped test run is refused; there it slipped through because it was not seen as a test.\n\n4. After you told it, the agent added a reader go-test for "scripts/dev go test*" itself (process 9506), so that command has one now. The gaps stay: a project wrapper around a test command is not recognised, and an unknown command piped through a filter gets no warning.\n\nPossible fixes, for you to decide: let a project declare its wrapper scripts (scripts/dev counts as what it runs), or classify by the words after a known wrapper; and warn on an unknown command piped into head or grep.',
     'What I found in the code-commandments journal, without changing anything:\n\n1. **The journal never asked for a reader.** Its reader requests table is empty, so no request was ever made there.\n2. **Why: the Go test run wasn\'t seen as a test run.** The command was `scripts/dev go test ... 2>&1 | grep ... | head -1`. The journal recognises `go test`, but here it was wrapped in the project\'s own script (`scripts/dev`), so it was classed as unknown. Reader requests, the test queue and the "no pipe on a test" rule only apply to test runs and builds, so none of them fired.\n3. **The pipe made it worse.** `grep` and `head` cut the output to one line, so there was nothing to read and no progress to show. In this project a piped test run is refused; there it slipped through because it wasn\'t seen as a test.\n4. **After you told it,** the agent added a reader for `scripts/dev go test*` itself, so that command has one now.\n\nThe gaps remain:\n- a project\'s wrapper script around a test command isn\'t recognised as a test run\n- an unknown command piped through a filter gets no warning\n\nPossible fixes, for you to decide:\n- **Wrapper scripts:** let a project declare its wrappers, or classify a command by the words after a known wrapper.\n- **Piped filters:** warn when an unknown command is piped into `head` or `grep`.'),
